iction_tokens (int positive), parseable (bool). If any missing or invalid types, raise ValueError.\n3. Compute for each call:\n   gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call\n   if prediction_tokens <= 0: raise ValueError\n   normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens\n   accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\n   Append to diagnostics dict.\n   If accepted, append call_id to accepted_call_ids list.\n4. Return result.\n\nWe'll ensure no mutation of case.\n\nNow, produce final JSON response.\n\nNote: The output must be a single JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing Python source code. That string must be valid JSON string, so we need to escape backslashes, quotes, newlines. In Python, we can write a triple-quoted string and then use json.dumps to escape properly. But we are constructing the response manually. I'll write the code as a multi-line string and then escape it. But to avoid mistakes, I'll write the code in a clean way and then manually escape. However, better to use a programmatic approach in mind. Since I'm writing directly, I'll write the code with \\n for newlines and \\\" for quotes.\n\nI'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case.get('threshold')\\n    if threshold is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing threshold')\\n    calls = case.get('calls')\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Missing or invalid calls')\\n    ...\"}\n\nBut need to escape properly. Use json.dumps in mind. I'll write the code as a Python string and then use a simple manual escaping. Since this is response, I'll produce the final JSON with the implementation string properly escaped.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case.get('threshold')\n    if threshold is None:\n        raise ValueError('Missing threshold')\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be number')\n    calls = case.get('calls')\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError('Calls must be a list')\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError('Call must be a dict')\n        call_id = call.get('call_id')\n        loss_without = call.get('loss_without_call')\n        loss_with = call.get('loss_with_call')\n        pred_tokens = call.get('prediction_tokens')\n        parseable = call.get('parseable')\n        if any(x is None for x in [call_id, loss_without, loss_with, pred_tokens, parseable]):\n            raise ValueError('Missing fields in call')\n        if not isinstance(pred_tokens, int) or pred_tokens <= 0:\n            raise ValueError('prediction_tokens must be positive int')\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)) or not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError('loss must be number')\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\n            raise ValueError('parseable must be bool')\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / pred_tokens\n        accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\n        diagnostics.append({\n            'accepted': accepted,\n            'call_id': call_id,\n            'normalized_gain': round(normalized_gain, 6)  # round to 6 decimals\n        })\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n    return {\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted_call_ids,\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\n    }\n\nNow, we need to wrap that in JSON. The code above is Python source. We need to escape it as a JSON string.\n\nWe'll produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value that string.\n\nI'll write it manually with proper escaping. For newlines, use \\n. For quotes, use \\\". For backslashes, use \\\\.\n\nBut note: inside the code, we have single quotes and double quotes. The JSON string value must be enclosed in double quotes, so we